WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.567 --> 00:00:01.567
-Yeah!

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:02.902
-Hey!
-Look at you, bud.

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:05.004
Thank you so much
for being here, man.

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:07.173
I love your quarantine hair.

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:08.941
-Thanks.
It's getting long.

00:00:08.941 --> 00:00:10.710
-Is this the longest
you've ever had it?

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.511
-This is, by far,
the longest I've ever had it.

00:00:12.511 --> 00:00:16.649
I was in ponytail --
I'm in ponytail territory.

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:19.252
Not to trigger people
about the ponytail.

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:21.187
It's gonna hang down
for now, but...

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:24.758
-[ Laughs ]
Where are you right now?

00:00:24.758 --> 00:00:26.892
-I'm at my girlfriend's house
in Miami,

00:00:26.892 --> 00:00:28.895
and that's her dog barking,
if you hear.

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:31.530
He's a Pomeranian.
This is home.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.733
This is home.
-Really?

00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:35.034
-Yeah.

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:38.237
-Have you been quarantining
with Camila's family?

00:00:38.237 --> 00:00:40.439
-Camila.
Yeah, I have been.

00:00:40.439 --> 00:00:45.945
And, you know, it's funny.
At first, when I got here,

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:47.580
it was, like, the beginning
of the album process,

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.349
and I was, like,
in a full panic state.

00:00:49.349 --> 00:00:50.984
I was like, "There's no way
I'm going to make music.

00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:52.819
This is gonna be impossible."
-Yep.

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:55.321
-But, like, a week goes by,
and you start cooking eggs

00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:57.456
in the morning,
and you start doing laundry

00:00:57.456 --> 00:01:00.326
and taking dogs for a walk,
and you have a coffee mug

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:01.928
that you've used the same one
every day,

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:04.430
and it just becomes,
like, really nice.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:05.831
-Yeah.
-Becomes nice.

00:01:05.831 --> 00:01:08.101
-I mean, I guess you've been --
-Which sounds so normal

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:10.369
for everyone else,
but it's very abnormal for me.

00:01:10.369 --> 00:01:13.239
-Well, you've been working
since you were 15

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:15.641
or something like that?
-I was 15,

00:01:15.641 --> 00:01:18.945
and I basically have been living
in a hotel room since I was 15.

00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:21.948
So, it's been nice, man.
It's been really nice.

00:01:21.948 --> 00:01:24.617
-I want to talk about
the new album, "Wonder."

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:27.520
I love the cover, by the way.
-Thanks.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.423
-It comes out December 4th,
but the single is out now,

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:32.926
and you're performing it
later tonight.

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:36.028
How did you come up
with the cover?

00:01:36.028 --> 00:01:40.633
-I mean, you know,
I was in Miami.

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:42.301
That's where I am
a lot of time now.

00:01:42.301 --> 00:01:45.705
So, I was in Miami,
and we were out on the water,

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:47.773
and I just got the final mix
of the song back,

00:01:47.773 --> 00:01:50.042
so I was playing it
on the boat that I was on.

00:01:50.042 --> 00:01:51.777
And all my --
Not all my friends.

00:01:51.777 --> 00:01:52.846
Like, three and four
of my friends

00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:54.347
and Camila were on the boat.

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:58.718
And the song was playing,
and we --

00:01:58.718 --> 00:01:59.985
It's just, like,
the skies opened up,

00:01:59.985 --> 00:02:01.987
and it started pouring down.
-Yeah.

00:02:01.987 --> 00:02:03.790
-This is, like,
my biggest fear is deep water.

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:06.392
So, we all jumped off the boat
in the middle of ocean.

00:02:06.392 --> 00:02:07.827
Probably was near land,

00:02:07.827 --> 00:02:09.762
but I'm just gonna say it was
in the middle of the ocean.

00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:11.764
But in the middle of the ocean,
and it was pouring rain,

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:14.334
and I looked around, and that
was kind of the essence.

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:17.470
So, you could say that
that was either done

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.606
in the middle of the ocean,
or in a parking lot in L.A.

00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:21.875
It's up to you
how you want to decide

00:02:21.875 --> 00:02:23.776
what that photo actually is.

00:02:23.776 --> 00:02:26.579
-I just know that feeling,
and I just think it's so cool.

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:28.481
It's, like,
so kind of free, almost.

00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:29.915
That's what it feels like.
-Totally.

00:02:29.915 --> 00:02:33.118
And, you know, I mean,
I feel like this time around,

00:02:33.118 --> 00:02:36.456
you know, I think
especially with the quarantine,

00:02:36.456 --> 00:02:39.258
I had a real moment
to find some stillness

00:02:39.258 --> 00:02:42.094
and just be quiet with myself,

00:02:42.094 --> 00:02:43.696
and also find some confidence

00:02:43.696 --> 00:02:46.565
with my work that I was doing,
you know?

00:02:46.565 --> 00:02:50.603
There was a real place
inside of me that was scared

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:53.572
of saying something wrong
or doing something wrong,

00:02:53.572 --> 00:02:55.541
or, like,
saying the wrong thing.

00:02:55.541 --> 00:02:59.145
And I think that,
after kind of chilling out

00:02:59.145 --> 00:03:01.313
and reflecting a bit, I found
this place where I realized,

00:03:01.313 --> 00:03:04.049
like, when we're talking about
matters of the heart,

00:03:04.049 --> 00:03:05.417
you don't have to be an expert.

00:03:05.417 --> 00:03:09.121
And the whole album
became about this freedom,

00:03:09.121 --> 00:03:11.657
this, like, thing of, like,
not being afraid

00:03:11.657 --> 00:03:14.360
of talking about things that you
morally know are right or wrong.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.063
And it allowed me
to just open up.

00:03:17.063 --> 00:03:19.199
And that's what
the whole video looks like.

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:20.232
That's what
the artwork looks like.

00:03:20.232 --> 00:03:21.700
That's what
the music sounds like.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:24.103
It's just --
It's internal freedom.

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:26.739
-I want to talk about the music,
'cause I think --

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:29.208
There's horns, there's
different sounds in here.

00:03:29.208 --> 00:03:31.944
I'm like, "Oh, I want to know
who inspired that."

00:03:31.944 --> 00:03:35.614
Like, "What was that?
What was that one?"

00:03:35.614 --> 00:03:41.053
-I came back off tour,
and my sister, who is 17 now,

00:03:41.053 --> 00:03:45.091
who's really cool, and she's,
like, way cooler than me now.

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:48.427
I mean, there's like -- for some
reason, when girls become 17,

00:03:48.427 --> 00:03:50.829
they're like aesthetically --
they know how to do everything,

00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:52.631
and they're just
the coolest person in the world.

00:03:52.631 --> 00:03:54.033
So, I was like,
"What are you listening to?"

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:57.437
And she played me Frankie Valli,
"Can't Take My Eyes Off of You."

00:03:57.437 --> 00:03:59.204
-Really?
-Yeah, totally.

00:03:59.204 --> 00:04:00.873
And it just kind of set me off.

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:05.645
And the whole album
was, like, brass, '60s drums.

00:04:05.645 --> 00:04:07.246
I got super into The Beach Boys.

00:04:07.246 --> 00:04:09.415
And my parents didn't listen to
a lot of The Beatles growing up,

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:11.083
so I started discovering
The Beatles really

00:04:11.083 --> 00:04:13.852
for the first time,
and it was pretty magical.

00:04:13.852 --> 00:04:16.322
So, that's kind of sonically
where it all came from.

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:20.927
-Is it intimidating when --
You're working on this album.

00:04:20.927 --> 00:04:23.562
Camila's there.
-Mm-hmm.

00:04:23.562 --> 00:04:26.098
-Do you go, like --
Is it embarrassing,

00:04:26.098 --> 00:04:27.533
or do you go like,
"All right, here's this song.

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:28.834
I think it's a good song."

00:04:28.834 --> 00:04:31.136
Or is it like,
"Can you help me on this?"

00:04:31.136 --> 00:04:32.538
Or what is it like?
-I mean, you have --

00:04:32.538 --> 00:04:35.775
You have, like, I think --
I think you have two choices.

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:37.843
You can either, like,
hide everything from her

00:04:37.843 --> 00:04:40.078
and just, like,
wait till the end type thing,

00:04:40.078 --> 00:04:42.715
and you can decide, or, like,
what I did was, like,

00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:46.785
from the very beginning,
I told her my thesis statement

00:04:46.785 --> 00:04:48.221
of what I wanted
the album to be.

00:04:48.221 --> 00:04:50.589
And it was the best thing I did,

00:04:50.589 --> 00:04:52.992
because there's always a point
halfway through a project

00:04:52.992 --> 00:04:54.927
where you're like,
"This is garbage.

00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:56.329
This is the worst thing
I've ever done.

00:04:56.329 --> 00:04:58.431
Everything is not --
It doesn't sound good.

00:04:58.431 --> 00:04:59.832
I don't know what I'm doing."

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:02.902
And that's when you need
that support to be like, "No.

00:05:02.902 --> 00:05:04.970
Stick with the idea,
because the idea is beautiful,

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:06.372
and it's gonna turn out."

00:05:06.372 --> 00:05:09.809
And I probably would've been
discouraged enough to, like,

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:11.811
move into
a different world, sonically,

00:05:11.811 --> 00:05:14.380
if I didn't have her kind of
backing me the whole time.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:16.215
So, I'm thankful for that.

00:05:16.215 --> 00:05:17.884
-Yeah, I think she knows
what she's doing, too.

00:05:17.884 --> 00:05:19.052
-She knows what she's doing.

